WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.840
SpaceX fired all 33 Raptor engines on super heavy, clearing the next step for Starship Flight 15. This is SpaceX Daily Episode 142. NASA's lunar calendar, a Florida campus expansion, and star shield going on to Air Force cargo jets, then the counterpoint and a deep dive. SpaceX released video of the engines lighting and holding on the launch mount. The super heavy booster stayed locked to the mount for the whole firing. The run followed cryoproof testing, a full cold load of the tanks on booster

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:35.660
23 and ship 43. It checks engine performance, propellant feed systems, and ground support

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:41.048
under flight-like conditions before the vehicle moves to the pad. NASA Administrator Jared

00:00:41.048 --> 00:00:46.208
Isaacman said Artemis III will spend 14 to 17 days in low-earth orbit next summer.

00:00:46.868 --> 00:00:51.188
The crew would practice sequential docking of Orion with prototype landers from SpaceX

00:00:51.188 --> 00:00:52.208
and Blue Origin.

00:00:52.988 --> 00:00:57.948
Biz Chosen reports the agency cited concerns over schedule slippage relative to China as

00:00:57.948 --> 00:01:00.728
the reason for accelerating private company participation.

00:01:01.328 --> 00:01:03.728
Jeremy Parsons, head of the Artemis program,

00:01:03.948 --> 00:01:06.508
described the test as the first time NASA will launch

00:01:06.508 --> 00:01:10.128
three spacecraft separately and verify integrated operations.

00:01:10.808 --> 00:01:12.768
Artemis III becomes a docking rehearsal

00:01:12.768 --> 00:01:15.128
rather than a landing, and Artemis IV

00:01:15.128 --> 00:01:16.968
is the 2028 landing attempt.

00:01:17.528 --> 00:01:19.308
Helium flow problems, the pressure

00:01:19.308 --> 00:01:22.688
int that pushes propellant and fuel leaks on the Artemis II rocket

00:01:22.688 --> 00:01:24.148
are what pushed the timeline back.

00:01:25.148 --> 00:01:27.528
The Roberts Road campus at Kennedy Space Center

00:01:27.528 --> 00:01:33.448
is the subject of a SpaceX filing to grow the site from 167 acres to nearly 400 acres.

00:01:34.048 --> 00:01:37.948
The extra land is meant to support starship operations ahead of Flight 16.

00:01:38.708 --> 00:01:43.308
Plans in the NASA spaceflight coverage include a new gigabay and project ignition ship

00:01:43.308 --> 00:01:49.028
Static Fire stands. A payload processing facility and a connecting road to NASA Parkway are part

00:01:49.028 --> 00:01:55.428
of the same package. Existing Hangar X2 currently handles dragon processing, Starlink payloads

00:01:55.428 --> 00:01:59.168
and tile production, and the first gigabay is already under construction.

00:01:59.808 --> 00:02:04.248
The expansion would add a star factory and more infrastructure so several starship vehicles

00:02:04.248 --> 00:02:06.268
can be worked at the Florida site at once.

00:02:07.373 --> 00:02:12.353
Ship 43 was seen turning on to Highway 4 at Starbase under Moonlight, according to Posts

00:02:12.353 --> 00:02:14.293
on X that the company has not confirmed.

00:02:14.893 --> 00:02:19.793
The upper stage kept rolling through Texas Ground Transport, lining up processing for Flight

00:02:19.793 --> 00:02:20.833
15 or 16.

00:02:20.833 --> 00:02:26.631
Boeing has started installing Starsheald on Air Force C-17 Globemaster III aircraft.

00:02:27.231 --> 00:02:33.011
Nine jets are already done at Port San Antonio, paid through two contracts worth $13.5 million

00:02:33.011 --> 00:02:34.911
dollars, according to the Express News.

00:02:35.511 --> 00:02:38.031
The program will equip 19 aircraft by March.

00:02:38.631 --> 00:02:42.631
The Air Force intends to outfit its full fleet of 222 Globemasters.

00:02:43.231 --> 00:02:47.531
Starsheald is the communications link for the airlift tanker-open-mission system suite,

00:02:47.531 --> 00:02:51.231
state, letting crews share mission data on global cargo and troop flights.

00:02:51.731 --> 00:02:56.211
Sierra Nevada Corporation supplies the star shield and A-TOMs kits used in the upgrades

00:02:56.211 --> 00:02:59.031
at Boeing's 1.6 million square-foot facility.

00:03:00.070 --> 00:03:05.770
Elon Musk disclosed that SpaceX has raised $40 billion in debt financing and predicted consolidation

00:03:05.770 --> 00:03:07.530
among competing cloud providers.

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:12.890
The standard reports the money supports capital needs across launch, satellite, and infrastructure

00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:13.630
programs.

00:03:13.630 --> 00:03:21.006
T-Mobile and Verizon asked the Federal Communications Commission, or FCC, to delay Starlink Mobile Approvals.

00:03:21.566 --> 00:03:27.026
Notebook Check reports the carrier's filed comments opposing accelerated licensing while SpaceX pushes

00:03:27.026 --> 00:03:31.726
terrestrial cell towers for direct-to-cell service, phones talking straight to satellites.

00:03:32.286 --> 00:03:37.806
The Filing's argue, extra-review is needed before the FCC grants more spectrum or operating authority.

00:03:37.806 --> 00:03:43.393
Earnings estimates for SpaceX moved higher at Irsta Group Bank after recent operational

00:03:43.393 --> 00:03:43.933
updates.

00:03:44.373 --> 00:03:49.153
Stronger visibility into launch cadence and Starlink subscriber trends is the basis market

00:03:49.153 --> 00:03:50.433
beat sites for the revision.

00:03:51.073 --> 00:03:56.073
Off the pad, NASA Space Flight reports the Starship Tower Arms reached full catch height during

00:03:56.073 --> 00:03:57.713
recent operations at Starbase.

00:03:58.773 --> 00:04:03.353
Worth keeping an eye on, Ukrainian officials rejected Elon Musk's assertion that commercial

00:04:03.353 --> 00:04:06.113
Starlink terminals were used for offensive drone strikes.

00:04:06.113 --> 00:04:11.613
They said speed limits and geographic restrictions already blocked those missions, and that satellite

00:04:11.613 --> 00:04:15.493
dependent operations run on the government-controlled star shield system instead.

00:04:16.093 --> 00:04:20.193
Kiev maintains that service restrictions disrupted air defense and drone coordination

00:04:20.193 --> 00:04:23.753
before speeds were restored, according to ABC45.

00:04:24.413 --> 00:04:29.113
Ukrainian and European partners are now exploring other satellite networks, including a proposed

00:04:29.113 --> 00:04:30.953
Nokia and ISY collaboration.

00:04:30.953 --> 00:04:36.252
The engineering deep dive today is the engine out math behind that super heavy firing.

00:04:36.892 --> 00:04:41.272
Early flight data established that lift-off remains achievable with three engines out, leaving

00:04:41.272 --> 00:04:46.092
30 operational because excess thrust margin grows as propellant mass falls.

00:04:46.852 --> 00:04:52.012
Isolation valves and independent turbo pump controls, the high speed pumps feeding each chamber,

00:04:52.532 --> 00:04:56.912
keep a single failure from cascading, so the remaining thrust vector stays intact.

00:04:56.912 --> 00:05:04.292
A methane oxygen booster is on a back-of-emvelope figure, about 90% propellant by mass at lift-off,

00:05:04.712 --> 00:05:07.492
which is why the same engines feel stronger every second.

00:05:08.112 --> 00:05:13.472
Sea level gravity is 9.8 meters per second squared, and large boosters typically leave

00:05:13.472 --> 00:05:16.552
the mount at a thrust to weight on the order of 1.2.

00:05:17.192 --> 00:05:20.352
Lose three engines and you shave roughly 9% of thrust.

00:05:20.872 --> 00:05:25.492
That ratio dips toward 1.1 at the start, then recovers as mass drops.

00:05:25.492 --> 00:05:32.532
Saturn V opened with 5 F1 engines in 1967. A reminder that engine count is only

00:05:32.532 --> 00:05:37.412
a risk if each chamber is a single point of failure. Later losses can be tolerated

00:05:37.412 --> 00:05:42.792
higher up, where air is thinner and side loads are lower. The ship upper stage inherits

00:05:42.792 --> 00:05:48.432
the idea after separation. Fewer engines, less mass, so one failed Raptor can still leave

00:05:48.432 --> 00:05:54.652
enough delta V, or change in velocity, to make orbit or deorbit. Abort triggers and thrust

00:05:54.652 --> 00:06:00.232
Vector control, steering by gambling nozzles, get rewritten so the vehicle can fly with one

00:06:00.232 --> 00:06:05.512
or two chambers offline. Hold down clamps keep the booster on the mount while tanks sit at flight

00:06:05.512 --> 00:06:11.732
pressure, exposing micro leaks and valve timing before anyone flies. Aerospace hardware often

00:06:11.732 --> 00:06:17.812
sits at an idiot index on the order of 10. Finish costs versus steel, copper, methane, and oxygen,

00:06:18.412 --> 00:06:23.752
and a lost booster is worse than a day of pad propellant. The full duration hold down also stress

00:06:23.752 --> 00:06:28.952
the flame trench and water day loose under a full propellant load. Sound and air moves about

00:06:28.952 --> 00:06:33.872
340 meters per second, so the tower takes the acoustic hit almost with the flame.

00:06:34.512 --> 00:06:39.272
The next integration step is verifying the load path from the booster hardpoints through the tower

00:06:39.272 --> 00:06:44.472
legs into the foundation, toward recovering the super heavy booster and the ship upper stage on

00:06:44.472 --> 00:06:52.066
the same flight. The stock finished up nearly 2%. Before we go, 19 Air Force C-17s are slated to

00:06:52.066 --> 00:06:56.106
carry star shield by March. That's the day at SpaceX, that's a wrap.

